For early career ASIC/RTL Engineers

Getting better at RTL design takes only two things: practice and patience. The best part is that you do not need a full SoC, an FPGA board or a semester-long project to learn powerful design skills. Small RTL modules teach more than people expect. A simple ALU or pipeline can give you a solid grasp of timing, handshakes, state machines and data-path control.

Here are ten small projects you can build in Verilog or System Verilog. Each comes with a short “How to get started” guide so you can begin immediately.

1. Build an 8-bit ALU
An ALU looks simple, but it teaches a surprising amount.

What you learn:
• Combinational logic
• Case statements
• Flag generation (zero, carry, overflow)
• Clean modular style

Press enter or click to view image in full size

How to get started:
• Write a module with inputs A, B, opcode
• Implement add, subtract, AND, OR, XOR
• Add flags like zero and carry
• Write a testbench that sweeps all opcodes
• Add signed arithmetic or shifts as an extension

2. Create a 4-stage pipeline
Take a small datapath and break it into stages.

What you learn:
• Pipeline registers
• Stage-by-stage timing
• Hazards and stalls
• Control versus datapath split

Press enter or click to view image in full size

How to get started:
• Start with a simple ALU datapath
• Define stages like fetch, decode, execute, write-back
• Insert a register between each stage
• Add stall logic and a small hazard condition
• Visualize the pipeline with waveform dumps

3. Design a FIFO
Every chip uses FIFOs. They are simple but deep.

What you learn:
• Pointer arithmetic
• Wraparound logic
• Off-by-one issues
• Full and empty conditions

Press enter or click to view image in full size

How to get started:
• Create read and write pointers
• Use a memory array as storage
• Implement full and empty checks
• Add almost full if you want a challenge
• Stress-test with random writes and reads

4. Implement a UART transmitter and receiver
A great introduction to protocols.

What you learn:
• FSM design
• Timing generation
• Serialization
• Protocol structure

